\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Declared values and sizes determine the one-edge polynomial}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{B} be a \vocabk{double-coset kernel}{fdip-012C}, with
value #{\beta_j} on the #{j}-th double coset, and let that
coset have size #{s_j}. Let #{\mathcal K} be a
\vocabk{class-function kernel}{fdip-012C}. On functions
constant on the double cosets, the
\vocabk{one-edge operator}{fdip-012C} has the following
matrix in the indicator basis. For #{g} in the #{m}-th
double coset, the entry from the #{j}-th coset to the
#{m}-th equals #{\beta_m\beta_j} times the sum of the values
#{\mathcal K(gx^{-1})} as #{x} runs through the #{j}-th
double coset. The sum does not depend on the choice of #{g}
in its coset. The characteristic polynomial, the determinant
of #{t{\bbI}} minus this matrix, is determined by the
declared values of #{B} and of #{\mathcal K} and by the
double-coset sizes. The rank equals the number of double
cosets, and \ref{fdip-013G} equates that number with the sum
of the squares of the invariant dimensions.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{e_j} be the indicator of the #{j}-th double coset.
Multiplication by #{B}, as in the definition of the
[one-edge operator](fdip-012C), sends #{e_j} to
#{\beta_j e_j}. For #{g} in the #{m}-th double coset,
##{(C_{\mathcal K}(\beta_j e_j))(g)
 =\beta_j\sum_{x\in G}\mathcal K(gx^{-1})e_j(x),}
and the one-edge operator at #{g} equals #{\beta_m} times
this value. The values lie in the commutative unital ring of
that definition. \ref{fdip-012D} shows that the operator
sends functions constant on double cosets to functions of the
same kind, so the sum is constant for #{g} in the #{m}-th
coset. The indicators form a basis, and these constants are
the matrix entries. Each sum runs through #{s_j} elements,
and each summand is a declared value of #{\mathcal K}. Each
entry is therefore determined by the declared values of both
kernels and by the size #{s_j}. The characteristic polynomial
is a polynomial in those entries. \ref{fdip-012D} gives the
rank, and \ref{fdip-013G} identifies it with the sum of the
squares of the invariant dimensions.}
}
